WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:21.132
I'm Eugenie Scott. I am the executive director of the National Center for Science Education.

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.899
We're in Oakland, California.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.399
We're a membership organization. Most of our members are scientists, teachers,

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.232
people concerned about church and state separation.

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:34.666
We monitor the creationism and evolution controversy,

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:39.232
and we try to help teachers and school boards and legislatures and citizens

00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:42.866
to cope with pressures against the teaching of evolution.

00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:49.466
So, in the 25 years or so that we've been doing this, we have found that there are

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:56.199
3 arguments that pretty much any creationist contention can be put into.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:01.832
If it's a video or a letter to the editor, a comment on a talk show, or whatever,

00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:05.466
it'll pretty much fall into one of these three categories.

00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:07.232
We call them the pillars of creationists.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:13.299
I'd like to tell you a little about the pillars, and why they're important to recognize,

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.132
because you as a scientist or a citizen who enjoys science...

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:22.432
who is interested in science, I hope is concerned about what's going on

00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:25.699
in American public schools, in the science classes there.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:32.999
Because at NCSE, we hear a lot about pressures from school boards upon curriculum.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.399
We hear pressures upon individual teachers by parents.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.899
We hear about pressures from school boards or state legislatures

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:42.299
that trickle down to the classroom teachers as well.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:48.699
And in all of these cases, there's been this continuing story for over thirty years

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.399
of people who don't want kids to learn evolution.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:57.366
And even though there have been various ins and outs of the creation and evolution controversy,

00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:00.899
and the creationists have always lost in court,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.966
this is an evolving target because creationism morphs, shall we say,

00:02:05.967 --> 00:02:09.166
in response to the legal environment. There's a natural selection going on here.

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:14.932
And, we have different forms of creationism, and the current form of creationism

00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:18.299
that we're coping with...that we're monitoring at NCSE today

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:24.599
is perhaps the most difficult to fight, because it doesn't appear to be

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.266
the kind of thing you can challenge directly in court.

00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:30.366
At any rate, I'll get to that in a moment.

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:33.332
So, let me tell you about the pillars of creationism

00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:38.099
because, as a concerned citizen, as a scientist, I hope you'd be able to recognize them

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:43.532
and understand the refutations of the pillars, so that you can hopefully

00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:46.166
contribute to your own community or state

00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:48.499
and help to improve science education.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:55.632
The first pillar is the pillar which challenges the science of evolution.

00:02:55.633 --> 00:03:00.066
The claim is often made that evolution is a theory in crisis...

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:02.866
that scientists are giving up on evolution...

00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:06.266
that more and more scientists are rejecting evolution as a failed theory.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:10.866
That will come as a surprise to scientists, but this is the claim that is made,

00:03:10.867 --> 00:03:14.266
and it's made very, very loudly on talk radios, on blogs,

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:16.799
on letters to the editor, and so forth.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.366
The second pillar of creationism is an ideological pillar.

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:26.466
It's the religious ideology that evolution is incompatible with my religious views,

00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:28.032
therefore evolution cannot be true.

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:34.566
And, this takes various forms. The strongest form is if an individual is a Biblical literalist

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:40.632
or a Quranic literalist. This point of view is present conservative Christians,

00:03:40.633 --> 00:03:43.132
conservative Muslims, and conservative Jews...

00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:46.366
the Middle Eastern model theisms, if you will.

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:52.432
The argument is made that my holy books... when I look at them, I see special creation.

00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:58.899
God created everything specially, in its present form, over a short period of time, 6000 years ago,

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.099
therefore evolution cannot be true.

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:05.432
And indeed, evolution is incompatible with that particular religious view.

00:04:05.433 --> 00:04:10.766
Some people reject evolution because of a human exceptionalism perspective...

00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:16.566
that human beings are so superior to all other organisms, God must have created us specially.

00:04:16.567 --> 00:04:22.799
There's a lot of ways that religion is used to oppose the teaching of evolution.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.932
The third pillar is the fairness pillar... it's a cultural concept, really.

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:34.132
It's a pillar where the anti-evolutionists are tapping into

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:38.466
some very deeply held American cultural positions:

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:47.266
fairness, balance, free speech, letting everyone speak, town hall meetings, all that sort of thing.

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:50.966
And, these are really great cultural values, and there's nothing wrong with it.

00:04:50.967 --> 00:04:55.099
It's just that they are misapplied, shall we say,

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.932
when it comes to how we decide what a kid should learn in school.

00:04:57.933 --> 00:05:02.999
Now, how can you, as a concerned citizen, deal with these three pillars?

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.699
As I say, whatever argument you hear, you can probably fit it into one of these three.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:13.032
So, if I could just give you the generic refutations, then you can go from there.

00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:20.432
There's plenty of evidence for the particulars on various websites -- on NCSE's website and others.

00:05:20.433 --> 00:05:25.966
The evolution is weak science pillar... let's take a look at that first.

00:05:25.967 --> 00:05:29.999
Well, the best way to refute that is to help your audience.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.132
Help the person you're talking to understand that, in fact,

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:37.366
the consensus in the scientific community...

00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:41.099
supporting the idea that evolution has happened, the universe is old, etc...

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.566
it is enormously strong.

00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:48.032
There have been many surveys of scientists... there's been a lot of survey research of scientists

00:05:48.033 --> 00:05:55.166
showing upwards of the high 90%s of scientists accept the idea of evolution.

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:58.766
It's hard to get scientists to agree on whether it's raining outside,

00:05:58.767 --> 00:06:03.699
but there's this really, really strong acceptance -- a consensus view

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.332
that evolution is valid science.

00:06:06.333 --> 00:06:07.599
But, the public doesn't know this.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.632
In the few surveys that have been done where members of public have been asked,

00:06:12.633 --> 00:06:16.099
"Do you think scientists agree that evolution has happened?"

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:22.199
the difference between what the public thinks scientists believe and what scientists actually believe

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:25.866
is like 20-30%. It's really a big disconnect.

00:06:25.867 --> 00:06:29.832
So, help the public understand that evolution is valid science.

00:06:29.833 --> 00:06:36.832
The second pillar -- the religion pillar -- is best dealt with by

00:06:36.833 --> 00:06:40.666
helping people understand that there's a very wide diversity of opinion

00:06:40.667 --> 00:06:42.066
within the Christian community.

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:46.399
I often joke that I think more evolution is taught

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.899
in Catholic parochial school than in public schools

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.766
because the Catholic church accepts something called theistic evolution,

00:06:53.767 --> 00:06:55.966
which is, of course evolution happened,

00:06:55.967 --> 00:07:00.766
but it was the way that God chose to bring about the world according to his plan.

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:04.566
There's varieties of theistic evolution out there, but it's basically an idea.

00:07:04.567 --> 00:07:09.399
It's a Christian theological idea that is fully compatible with what science shows us

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.332
about living things evolving through time.

00:07:12.333 --> 00:07:19.866
Catholics and mainstream Protestants official theology is, in fact, theistic evolution.

00:07:19.867 --> 00:07:25.366
So, it is not the case that if you are Christian, you have to reject evolution.

00:07:25.367 --> 00:07:28.899
Some Christian views, absolutely, but not all Christian views.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.766
Most people think there is a dichotomy.

00:07:30.767 --> 00:07:36.866
On one side is atheist evolution, on the other side is Christian creationism, but that's false.

00:07:36.867 --> 00:07:37.899
That's just empirically wrong.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.132
It's not a dichotomy. It's a continuum.

00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:48.732
The third pillar -- the fairness pillar -- is the most difficult one to deal with

00:07:48.733 --> 00:07:52.699
because it is so strongly rooted in American cultural traditions.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:56.766
The fairness pillar is best dealt with by helping the public

00:07:56.767 --> 00:08:00.432
think about this a little bit more carefully.

00:08:00.433 --> 00:08:04.466
Obviously, we want debate in our society about a lot of things.

00:08:04.467 --> 00:08:06.966
People should be free to express opinions.

00:08:06.967 --> 00:08:12.532
People should be free to argue vehemently about one position or another, one opinion or another,

00:08:12.533 --> 00:08:15.299
but that's not really what we're talking about.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.399
What we're really talking about is a much more narrow issue.

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.232
We're really talking about what you teach in high school science class.

00:08:23.233 --> 00:08:26.666
Because that is where these policies that come to school boards

00:08:26.667 --> 00:08:29.599
or the pressure that comes upon teachers really come to.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:34.632
What you should teach in high school science class is -- no surprise -- science.

00:08:34.633 --> 00:08:39.866
And, a high school science teacher has the very important job of picking

00:08:39.867 --> 00:08:45.099
from the huge array of subject matter in biology, or physics, or chemistry, or whatever.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:50.599
That smaller range of topics that is essential for young learners

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:54.799
who are just building an understanding of that particular scientific field.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:01.132
And in biology, one of those core principles is evolution --

00:09:01.133 --> 00:09:03.532
that living things are related genetically

00:09:03.533 --> 00:09:08.499
in this huge genealogical tree of living creatures through time.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:16.666
So, help the public understand that we're not talking about inhibiting anybody's free speech.

00:09:16.667 --> 00:09:21.199
We're not talking about science being dogmatic and forcing views upon the students.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:26.299
We're talking about doing the same thing in high school biology class as we do in physics,

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:32.499
chemistry, or for that matter, mathematics, English literature, and geography.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.799
We teach the consensus view of the field.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:38.799
Now, if the creationists or the intelligent design people,

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:41.366
or anybody else who challenges evolution

00:09:41.367 --> 00:09:45.299
wants to make the argument that they have valid science

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.832
that deserves to be taught in the public school classroom,

00:09:47.833 --> 00:09:49.966
knock yourself out.

00:09:49.967 --> 00:09:52.899
But, your job is to convince the scientific community first.

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:59.566
What is happening is that, because of the politicized nature of American education,

00:09:59.567 --> 00:10:02.832
curriculum decisions are made by elected officials,

00:10:02.833 --> 00:10:06.099
and elected officials are looking over their shoulders at who votes,

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:11.132
as opposed to necessarily paying attention to what's the best curriculum.

00:10:11.133 --> 00:10:14.332
Because American education is politicized,

00:10:14.333 --> 00:10:21.266
there are pressures upon teachers outside of science to change the curriculum one way or the other.

00:10:21.267 --> 00:10:23.566
They should be protected from that.

00:10:23.567 --> 00:10:31.199
Students should not be the platform upon which we fight the culture wars.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.099
Let the creationists, or the intelligent design people, or the anti-evolutionists

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:39.132
demonstrate to the scientific community that they have valid views,

00:10:39.133 --> 00:10:42.899
that evolution is either weak science, or that they've got a better alternative,

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:47.499
and it'll trickle down to the high school if it makes it into the consensus.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:55.766
They argue fairness. Frankly, to teach students that something is not valid

00:10:55.767 --> 00:10:58.266
when there's such a strong scientific consensus about it

00:10:58.267 --> 00:11:00.866
is about the most unfair thing you can do.

00:11:00.867 --> 00:11:05.966
I would hope that being familiar with the refutations of the three pillars

00:11:05.967 --> 00:11:08.699
would give you more confidence

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:11.499
in standing up for good science in your community.

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:17.232
If you hear of a school board issue, or some state legislation, or some school board legislation,

00:11:17.233 --> 00:11:20.232
or if you even hear a word that one of your local teachers

00:11:20.233 --> 00:11:22.699
is reluctant to teach evolution because

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:25.899
she or he feels that there's pressure from the community,

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.299
I hope that you will stand up for good science.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:33.099
And the first thing you can do is contact us at NCSE.com.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:36.932
We can put you in touch with people in your community who feel likewise,

00:11:36.933 --> 00:11:41.266
we can give you the ammunition, shall we say, the arguments that you can use,

00:11:41.267 --> 00:11:47.332
and the methods that you can use to make a good argument to your local decision makers.

00:11:47.333 --> 00:11:48.799
Because teachers need help.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:56.299
We get calls every week from teachers who want some advice, who want some support

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:59.599
to teach good science. And they deserve all the support

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.632
that we as scientists and concerned citizens can give them.

00:12:02.633 --> 01:00:00.000
Thank you.

